# config/Kconfig.security — Secure boot, link encryption, PMP, stack hardening
#
# Maps to cargo features `secure-boot-enforced` and
# `link-provisioning-enforced`.

menu "Security"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secure boot
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED
    # cargo-feature: secure-boot-enforced
    bool "Enforce Ed25519 signature verification at boot" if !BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE
    default y if !BUILD_TYPE_DEV
    default n
    help
      Default: y on every build except BUILD_TYPE_DEV (release-safe;
      the QEMU board is a development build, the boards are release
      builds). A development build keeps the TEST key and unsigned boot.
      When enabled, the bootloader verifies the kernel image signature
      using the Ed25519 public key embedded at build time (crates/core/ota, was SECURE_BOOT_KEY_FP -- removed 2026-09-26, V2.6, never consumed)
      before jumping to _start.  If the signature is absent or invalid,
      boot halts unconditionally.
      Disable only for development.  MUST be y for any production
      deployment — enforced by cargo feature `secure-boot-enforced`.
      A BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE build has it on and cannot turn it off.

config LINK_PROVISIONING_ENFORCED
    # cargo-feature: link-provisioning-enforced
    bool "Brain link provisioning: no boot without a link key, no UART bridge"
    default n
    depends on USE_ROBOT
    help
      Robot domain only (wave 11): the brain link exists only there.
      The brain's TCP link is always a Noise session (ChaCha20-Poly1305,
      keys from a per-connection handshake): there is no plaintext mode and
      no MAC-only mode in any build, and a key in CONFIG.INI cannot change
      that.  This option hardens what surrounds it:
        * boot halts when the link key (the reserved sector the USB
          mass-storage gadget cannot address) is missing or invalid, instead
          of going on with the link down;
        * the handshake is refused while the entropy pool is unseeded, so
          no session is ever keyed from timing alone;
        * the UART bridge, which carries brain-protocol frames (including
          motion commands) outside any session, is down in both directions.
      A keyless encrypted-only robot can never talk, so the failure
      surfaces at provisioning, not in the field.
      Enforced by cargo feature `link-provisioning-enforced`, compile-time
      only, no runtime override, mirroring SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED.
      Note the trade-off: a unit that loses its key will not boot.

config OTA_SIG_MANDATORY
    bool "OTA images must be signed (refuse unsigned updates)" if !BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE
    default y if !BUILD_TYPE_DEV
    default y if !SECURITY_GENERAL
    default n
    help
      Default: y on every build except BUILD_TYPE_DEV (release-safe),
      whatever the SECURITY posture. When y, the OTA subsystem rejects any firmware image that does
      not carry a valid Ed25519 signature file (KERN_*.SIG on FAT).
      Recommended: set to y whenever SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED = y so
      the signing chain is unbroken end-to-end.  A BUILD_TYPE_DEV build
      starts on only for the SECURITY postures but general; a
      BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE build has it on and cannot turn it off.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PMP / memory protection
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config PMP_REGIONS_RESERVED
    int "PMP regions reserved for M-mode kernel use"
    range 0 12
    default 4
    help
      Number of RISC-V PMP entries the kernel allocates for its own
      regions (ROM, MMIO, kernel text/data).  The remaining
      (16 - PMP_REGIONS_RESERVED) entries are available for user-process
      isolation.  Must leave headroom; current hardware cap is 16.
      Current codebase: 6 total in use, but 4 is the design budget
      (crates/core/arch-riscv64/src/ext/pmp.rs N_PMP_REGIONS = 6 current impl).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signed capability topology (wave 15 TOPOSIGN)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

choice
    prompt "Capability topology source"
    default TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_SIGNED_REQUIRED if !BOARD_QEMU
    default TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_SIGNED_OR_BUILTIN
    help
      Where the boot takes the capability topology from: the rows every
      ring-3 task's capabilities, scheduler class, priority and memory
      budget come from. The signed form is /fat/CAPS.TOM + /fat/CAPS.SIG
      (a bare Ed25519 signature under the embedded topology key, the key
      CONFIG.SIG uses) and /fat/SCHED.TOM, which CAPS.TOM binds by its
      SHA-256 (`sched_sha256`), together with this device's id and a
      counter (TOPOLOGY_BIND_DEVICE, TOPOLOGY_COUNTER_FLOOR). The built-in
      form is crates/core/topology/src/builder.rs, compiled into the image.
      `make topo-volume TOPO_IMAGE=<img>` writes the built-in topology of a
      given .config and feature set onto an image, bound to that image's
      device (tests/host/topology-tests topo_emit).

      CAPS.SIG is verified before any byte is parsed, and SCHED.TOM's hash
      before SCHED.TOM is parsed; the parsed topology then passes the same
      admission as the built-in one (admission_check, deadline_admission on
      the real CPU count, the RT_BAND_CAP_PCT band, memory) BEFORE it is
      published, so a refused file never becomes the topology.

      Defaults (owner decision, 2026-10-05): "signed required" on every
      product board (BOARD_VF2, BOARD_K1, BOARD_GENERIC: the vf2, k1,
      embedded and fleet profiles), because under signed-or-built-in
      deleting the files from the (USB-exposed) volume boots the built-in
      topology, which may grant more than the deployment's signed one.
      Every product make target builds the volume that carries them,
      signed with the board key: `make vf2` -> $AZOS_OUT/build/disk-board.img,
      `make k1` -> $AZOS_OUT/build/disk-board-k1.img, `make build-fleet` ->
      $AZOS_OUT/build/disk-board-fleet.img; without TOPOLOGY_PRIVKEY_PATH they
      refuse. "Signed if present" on BOARD_QEMU (development and the gate),
      where one disk is booted by kernels with different built-in
      topologies.

      Cost, instructions at the topology install (QEMU virt, -icount
      shift=0, -smp 1, the gate's 19-row topology, 2026-10-05),
      riscv64 / aarch64:
        built-in only (BUILTIN)                        17,800 / 16,376
        no files on the volume (fallback + record)    126,400 / 116,141
        signed set installed (one signature)        1,797,300 / 1,271,881
      The one Ed25519 verification is about 1.35 M of it on riscv64
      (measured with the verification skipped: 443,400); reads, the
      SCHED.TOM hash, the binding, the floor sector, the parse and the
      admission of the candidate are the rest. A first boot of a new
      counter adds the floor write. RAM: the two buffers (see
      TOPOLOGY_CAPS_MAX_KB), under every choice. The cost is printed on the
      "[TOPO] Topology installed: ... in N ticks" line of every boot.

    config TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_BUILTIN
        bool "Built-in only (never read the volume)"
        help
          The topology compiled into the image, as before wave 15. The
          files are not opened: no boot cost, no RAM for the buffers
          (they are still reserved), and no record.

    config TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_SIGNED_OR_BUILTIN
        bool "Signed if present and valid, else built-in"
        help
          The signed topology when all four files are on the volume and
          it verifies, parses and is admitted. With none of the four
          files, the built-in topology, a "[TOPO] WARNING" line and a
          SAFETY_TOPO_SOURCE flight-recorder record (action 1, fallback
          missing). A set that is present but refused (partial, too
          large, bad signature, does not parse, not admitted) is a
          security event: see TOPOLOGY_INVALID.

    config TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_SIGNED_REQUIRED
        bool "Signed required (halt without it)"
        help
          The signed topology or no boot. Missing or refused files write
          a durable SAFETY_TOPO_SOURCE record (action 3 missing, 4
          invalid) and halt the boot hart before any ring-3 task or
          kernel task the topology admits is created. Choose it only
          when every volume this image boots from carries the signed
          files.

endchoice

choice
    prompt "A signed topology that is present but invalid"
    depends on TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_SIGNED_OR_BUILTIN
    default TOPOLOGY_INVALID_FALLBACK
    help
      Under "signed if present and valid": what a set that IS on the
      volume but is refused does. Either way the boot prints a "[TOPO]
      REFUSED" line with the reason and writes a durable
      SAFETY_TOPO_SOURCE record whose detail says which file and step.

      Default fallback: whoever can tamper with the files can also delete
      them, and deletion already boots the built-in topology under this
      source policy, so halting on a tampered file would add a denial of
      service without closing anything. Halt is for a deployment that
      wants a corrupted or tampered volume to stop the machine where it
      can be inspected (with deletion still falling back; for "never
      boot without the signed topology" choose "signed required").

    config TOPOLOGY_INVALID_FALLBACK
        bool "Fall back to the built-in topology"
        help
          Install the built-in topology after the REFUSED line and the
          record (action 2, fallback invalid).

    config TOPOLOGY_INVALID_HALT
        bool "Halt"
        help
          Record (action 4, halt invalid) and halt the boot hart.

endchoice

config TOPOLOGY_BIND_DEVICE
    bool "The signed topology must name this device"
    depends on !TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_BUILTIN
    default y
    help
      CAPS.TOM (format 4) must carry `device = "<32 hex>"` equal to this
      device's id: the device record in reserved tail sector 2
      (tools/device_provision.py), the id CONFIG.SIG v2 is bound to. A file
      signed for another device is refused (SAFETY_TOPO_SOURCE detail step
      8), a file with no `device` key too (step 10), and so is any file on a
      device with no record (step 11). With n, `device` is optional, and
      still checked when present.

      Default y for every profile: `make` writes the image's own id into the
      files it signs (QEMU images and board volumes alike). Cost: a 16-byte
      compare and one sector read at boot (the record is already read for
      CONFIG.INI; it is read again here), either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_COUNTER_FLOOR
    bool "Refuse a signed topology older than the last one accepted"
    depends on !TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_BUILTIN
    default y
    help
      CAPS.TOM (format 4) must carry `counter = N` (N >= 1), and N must be at
      least the floor this device keeps in reserved tail sector
      TOPOLOGY_FLOOR_SECTOR. Accepting a file with a higher counter raises
      the floor (one sector write and a flush, once per new counter). An
      older, validly signed CAPS.TOM put back on the volume is refused as a
      replay (SAFETY_TOPO_SOURCE detail step 9, low bits the counter). The
      CONFIG.SIG v2 semantics: the floor only moves up, and a missing or
      invalid floor record reads as 0.

      What it does not stop: whoever can rewrite the reserved tail (a card
      reader, not the USB export) can reset the floor.

      Default y for every profile. Cost: one sector read per boot, either
      ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_FLOOR_SECTOR
    int "Reserved tail sector of the topology counter floor"
    range 3 7
    default 3
    help
      Index in the reserved tail past the FAT32 volume
      (kernel/src/msc_gadget.rs, 8 sectors) of the "KTPF" floor record
      (crates/core/topology/src/device_record.rs). 0 is the link key, 1 the
      entropy seed, 2 the device record; 3..7 are free. Change it only to
      move it off a sector another feature of your build claims, and give
      tools/device_provision.py --topo-sector the same value.

config BOOT_INI_MAX_KB
    int "Largest CONFIG.INI the boot reads (KiB)"
    range 1 64
    default 2 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 4
    help
      The buffer /fat/CONFIG.INI is read into before its CONFIG.SIG is
      checked (kernel/src/boot/config_auth.rs). A larger file is refused
      unread with its own reason, "CONFIG.INI larger than
      BOOT_INI_MAX_KB" (AuthorityLoss::TooLarge, record code 10): it is
      never verified over its first bytes, which would fail as a bad
      signature and read as tampering. On a provisioned device it latches
      the e-stop as any other loss of config authority does.
      tools/gen_config_sig.py refuses to sign a file over the same limit.

      Cost: this many KiB of .bss. The store holds at most 32 entries of a
      24-byte key and a 48-byte value (crates/core/config), about 2.4 KB
      of text without comments.

config TOPOLOGY_CAPS_MAX_KB
    int "Largest CAPS.TOM the boot reads (KiB)"
    range 1 4096
    default 8   if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 16  if (HWCLASS_SBC || HWCLASS_DESKTOP)
    default 256 if HWCLASS_SERVER
    help
      The buffer CAPS.TOM is read into (kernel/src/boot/topology.rs). It
      stays allocated for the life of the kernel: every task name, class
      name and grant target of the installed topology borrows from it. A
      larger file is refused unread (SignedRefusal::TooLarge).

      Cost: this many KiB of .bss on riscv64 and aarch64 alike, whether or
      not the volume carries the file (also under TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_BUILTIN).
      Size it from your file: the QEMU gate's topology (19 rows, 53
      grants, its test rows included) is 4.6 KB; a row is about 100 bytes
      plus 50 per grant. Fleet's 256 KiB leaves room for about 2,000 rows.

config TOPOLOGY_SCHED_MAX_KB
    int "Largest SCHED.TOM the boot reads (KiB)"
    range 1 1024
    default 2  if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 4  if (HWCLASS_SBC || HWCLASS_DESKTOP)
    default 16 if HWCLASS_SERVER
    help
      The buffer SCHED.TOM is read into; kept for the kernel's life (the
      class names borrow from it). A larger file is refused unread.

      Cost: this many KiB of .bss, either ISA. The QEMU gate's SCHED.TOM
      (5 classes) is under 1 KB; a class is about 190 bytes.

config TOPOLOGY_NEVERALLOW
    bool "Refuse a topology that breaks a neverallow rule"
    default y
    help
      The rules of crates/core/topology/src/neverallow.rs state grants no
      topology may make, whatever its file says: no ring-3 row holds an
      actuator unless the table lists the row as one, no desktop image
      grants a motor, no row both asks the name server for names and serves
      an endpoint or a driver. A topology that breaks one is refused by
      `admission_check` (the signed candidate falls back or halts as
      TOPOLOGY_SOURCE says; the built-in topology halts) and a decision
      record (`neverallow`, `why list`) names the rule and the row.
      `make check-neverallow` runs the same rules over every preset's
      topology, with this option ignored.

      With n the rules are not applied at boot or by `admission_check`.
      Cost: one pass over the rows' grants at install, either ISA, and a
      few hundred bytes of rule table.

config TOPOLOGY_DRIVETRAIN
    bool "The built-in topology may declare the drivetrain"
    depends on !USE_DESKTOP
    default y
    help
      With the `profile-actuation` fixture (the QEMU board's), the
      built-in topology grants `motor.0` and `motor.1` to the row that
      drives the robot. A desktop image never does: the neverallow rule
      `desktop-grants-no-motor` refuses a topology that grants a motor
      there, so this option is hidden (and n) under USE_DESKTOP rather
      than leaving the combination to be refused at boot.

      Cost: none; two grants of the autorun row, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_CLASSES
    int "Scheduler classes a topology may declare"
    range 6 255
    default 8
    help
      Size of the class table of the topology
      (crates/core/topology/src/types.rs, MAX_CLASSES). A SCHED.TOM with
      more classes is refused by the parser. The class count is kept in a
      u8, hence the range.

      Cost: 32 B of RAM per class (the topology slot, .data) and 132 B of
      stack per class in deadline admission (a u32 per class for each of
      its 32 admission CPUs, plus the budget), either ISA. The built-in
      topology declares 6 (the five built-in classes and `session`), hence
      the lower bound.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_PIPELINES
    int "[pipeline.NAME] sections a topology may declare"
    range 1 255
    default 8
    help
      Size of the pipeline table (MAX_PIPELINES): each declared pipeline
      adds its dma_kb to the boot DMA pool (DMA_POOL_FLOOR_KB). More are
      refused. Kept in a u8.

      Cost: 24 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_DEVICE_ROWS
    int "[device.NAME] rows a topology may declare"
    range 1 255
    default 16 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 32
    help
      Size of the device row table (MAX_DEVICE_ROWS, CAPS.TOML format 6):
      each `[device.NAME]` row selects hardware the bus detects (`match =
      "pci:1af4:1041"`, `"dt:ns16550a"`, `"virtio:2"`, `"pciclass:010802:
      ffffff"`) and says what to do with it: admit it, admit it with another
      native driver (`driver = "..."`), or refuse it (`admit = false`). With
      HWBUS_AUTOBIND_TOPOLOGY a device no row admits is detected and not
      bound. More rows are refused. Kept in a u8.

      Cost: 96 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_IMPORTS
    int "Names one task row may import from the name server"
    range 1 64
    default 8
    help
      The `imports = [...]` list of a [task.NAME] row (CAPS.TOML format 5):
      the endpoint names the name server (NAMESD.ELF) may hand that row. A
      row with more is refused by the parser. The list is the row's whole
      claim on the name space: a name it does not list is not answered.

      Cost: none by itself; the pool is TOPOLOGY_IMPORTS_TOTAL.

config TOPOLOGY_IMPORTS_TOTAL
    int "Imports declared across all rows of a topology"
    range 1 4096
    default 32
    help
      Size of the imports pool (types.rs, IMPORTS_TOTAL): every name any row
      imports takes one entry. More are refused. The built-in topology
      declares 1 (the shell imports the terminal).

      Cost: 16 B of RAM per entry (a name slice, .data), either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_SIGNERS
    int "Package signers one topology may admit"
    range 1 64
    default 4
    help
      The `[signer.NAME]` sections of a format-6 CAPS.TOM: the device policy
      of signed application packages (crates/core/pkg). A package installs
      only when its author's key is one of these; a topology with more is
      refused by the parser. The policy is signed with the rest of the
      topology, so the device owner's key is the only root of trust and a
      package cannot add a signer for itself.

      Cost: 80 B of RAM per entry (.bss in the installer, .data in the
      kernel), either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_SIGNER_WARRANTS_TOTAL
    int "Warrant ceilings declared across all signers"
    range 1 1024
    default 32
    help
      Size of the pool behind the signers' `warrants = ["kind:prefix", ...]`
      lists (types.rs, SIGNER_WARRANTS_TOTAL): the ceiling of what each
      signer's packages may ask for. A package row holding a grant no entry
      of its signer covers is refused. More entries than the pool holds are
      refused by the parser.

      Cost: 16 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.
config TOPOLOGY_MAX_PEERS
    int "[peer.NAME] sections (classes of remote nodes) a topology may declare"
    range 1 16
    default 4
    help
      Size of the peer table (types.rs, MAX_PEERS), CAPS.TOML format 6: a
      class is a set of node ids an `export` of the RPC server's row may name
      (`export = ["decisions@fleet"]`). More are refused.

      Cost: 50 B plus 36 B per TOPOLOGY_PEER_NODES of RAM per class (the
      nodes, their pinned Noise keys and the measurement), either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_PEER_NODES
    int "Nodes one peer class may list"
    range 1 16
    default 4
    help
      The `nodes = [...]` list of a [peer.NAME] section. A node belongs to one
      class only. More are refused. Cost: 36 B per node per class (the node
      id and its pinned Noise key), either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_EXPORTS_TOTAL
    int "Export entries across the RPC server's row"
    range 1 256
    default 16
    help
      Size of the exports pool (types.rs, EXPORTS_TOTAL): every NAME@CLASS of
      the `export` key takes one entry. More are refused. Nothing leaves a
      node except through one of these entries.

      Cost: 48 B of RAM per entry (three name slices), either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_POLICIES
    int "[policy.NAME] rows (limits on a Domain and its subtree) a topology may declare"
    range 1 64
    default 4 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 8
    help
      Size of the policy table (types.rs, MAX_POLICIES), CAPS.TOML format 7:
      each `[policy.NAME]` row limits what a task row's Domain (and, with
      `scope = "subtree"`, every Domain created beneath it) may create:
      endpoints, ports, MemObjs above a size. The parent's limit holds for
      every descendant, whatever the descendant is granted. More rows are
      refused.

      Cost: 40 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_LIVEPATCHES
    int "[livepatch.NAME] rows (signed kernel patches the device admits) a topology may declare"
    range 1 32
    default 2 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 4
    help
      Size of the livepatch table (types.rs, MAX_LIVEPATCHES), CAPS.TOML
      format 7: each `[livepatch.NAME]` row admits ONE patch blob, named by the
      SHA-256 of its bytes, for one ISA, bound to a kernel build-id and a
      security epoch. A blob no row names is refused, so the signed topology
      is the allowlist of what may be written into the kernel's text; a
      certifiable profile admits none. More rows are refused.

      Cost: 96 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_LEAP_ENTRIES
    int "Leap-second entries the [clock] row of a topology may declare"
    range 1 64
    default 8 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 32
    help
      Size of the TAI-UTC table (types.rs, LEAP_ENTRIES), CAPS.TOML format 7:
      `leaps = ["<UTC unix seconds>:<TAI-UTC after>", ...]` in the `[clock]`
      row. TAI is UTC plus this offset, and the signed table is the device's
      only source of it until a time source (NTP/PTP) announces a newer one.
      The table in force since 1972 has 28 entries. More are refused.

      Cost: 16 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_SWAPS
    int "[swap.NAME] rows (swap areas on a partition) a topology may declare"
    range 1 4
    default 1
    help
      Size of the swap table (types.rs, MAX_SWAPS), CAPS.TOML format 7: each
      `[swap.NAME]` row names the partition (1 to 16, the block layer's
      partition table) that holds a swap area. A partition is used by one row
      of the swap and volume tables together. More rows are refused.

      Cost: 24 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_VOLUMES
    int "[volume.NAME] rows (native volumes to mount) a topology may declare"
    range 1 16
    default 2 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 4
    help
      Size of the volume table (types.rs, MAX_VOLUMES), CAPS.TOML format 7:
      each `[volume.NAME]` row names the partition that holds an azvol native
      volume, where to mount it, whether it is read-only, whether its blocks
      are encrypted at rest and, for a read-only system image, the verity root
      hash it must match. A partition is used by one row of the swap and
      volume tables together. More rows are refused.

      Cost: 88 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_LOGINS
    int "[login.NAME] rows (SSH logins) a topology may declare"
    range 1 4
    default 2 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 4
    help
      Size of the login table (types.rs, MAX_LOGINS), CAPS.TOML format 7:
      each `[login.NAME]` row is one account of the SSH server (SSHD): NAME
      is the user name `ssh` is given, and `ssh_key` lists the ssh-ed25519
      public keys (TOPOLOGY_LOGIN_KEYS at most) that log in as it. The rows
      are in the signed topology, so adding a key is a new signed topology
      and not a file on the volume. The kernel shows the rows to the SSH
      server and the login server in their view region (`inspect.logins`:
      a user name and a 32-byte key per node, MAX_LOGINS * LOGIN_KEYS of
      them, sized at boot). More rows are refused.

      Cost: 80 B of RAM per entry and key, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_LOGIN_KEYS
    int "ssh_key entries one [login.NAME] row may list"
    range 1 4
    default 2
    help
      Public keys one login may have (types.rs, LOGIN_KEYS): a person with a
      laptop and a phone has two. More are refused.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_GUESTS
    int "[guest.NAME] rows (virtual machines the device may run) a topology may declare"
    range 1 16
    default 1 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 2
    help
      Size of the guest table (types.rs, MAX_GUESTS), CAPS.TOML format 7: each
      `[guest.NAME]` row declares one guest: its virtual CPUs, its memory, the
      device backends it is given and the envelope its effects are held to. A
      guest holds no capability of its own; the ring-3 backends that serve its
      devices do. More rows are refused.

      Cost: 40 B plus 16 B per TOPOLOGY_GUEST_DEVICES of RAM per entry, either
      ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_GUEST_DEVICES
    int "Device backends one guest row may list"
    range 1 16
    default 8
    help
      The `devices = [...]` list of a [guest.NAME] row: the backends
      (`virtio-blk`, `virtio-net`, `virtio-console`, ...) the guest is given.
      A row with more is refused.

      Cost: 16 B per device per guest, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_ROUTES
    int "[route.NAME] rows (static routes) a topology may declare"
    range 1 64
    default 8 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 16
    help
      Size of the route table (types.rs, MAX_ROUTES), CAPS.TOML format 7: each
      `[route.NAME]` row is one static route, a destination prefix (IPv4 or
      IPv6) reached on an interface, directly or through a gateway, at a
      metric. A default route learned from DHCP is not a row. More rows are
      refused.

      Cost: 88 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_FIREWALL_ROWS
    int "[firewall.NAME] rows (packet filter rules) a topology may declare"
    range 1 128
    default 16 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 32
    help
      Size of the firewall table (types.rs, MAX_FIREWALL_ROWS), CAPS.TOML
      format 7: each `[firewall.NAME]` row is one rule of the packet filter,
      a direction, a protocol, a port or port range, a remote address prefix
      and an action. Rules are tried in file order and the first match wins.
      More rows are refused.

      Cost: 72 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_TSN
    int "[tsn.NAME] rows (TSN admissions) a topology may declare"
    range 1 16
    default 2 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 4
    help
      Size of the TSN table (types.rs, MAX_TSN), CAPS.TOML format 8: each
      `[tsn.NAME]` row admits real-time traffic on one network device with a
      Qbv gate window and a link budget.
      More rows are refused.

      Cost: 40 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_FIELDBUS
    int "[fieldbus.NAME] rows (fieldbus masters) a topology may declare"
    range 1 16
    default 2 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 4
    help
      Size of the fieldbus table (types.rs, MAX_FIELDBUS), CAPS.TOML format 8:
      each `[fieldbus.NAME]` row runs one EtherCAT, CANopen or Modbus master on a
      device.
      More rows are refused.

      Cost: 40 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_AUDIO
    int "[audio.NAME] rows (audio streams) a topology may declare"
    range 1 16
    default 2 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 4
    help
      Size of the audio table (types.rs, MAX_AUDIO), CAPS.TOML format 8: each
      `[audio.NAME]` row is one stream on one codec or controller.
      More rows are refused.

      Cost: 40 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_DISPLAYS
    int "[display.NAME] rows (panels) a topology may declare"
    range 1 16
    default 1 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 4
    help
      Size of the display table (types.rs, MAX_DISPLAYS), CAPS.TOML format 8:
      each `[display.NAME]` row is one panel the compositor drives.
      More rows are refused.

      Cost: 40 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_USERS
    int "[user.NAME] rows (human users) a topology may declare"
    range 1 16
    default 1 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 4
    help
      Size of the user table (types.rs, MAX_USERS), CAPS.TOML format 8: each
      `[user.NAME]` row is one user, with a root directory warrant and a
      keystore.
      More rows are refused.

      Cost: 48 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_KEYS
    int "[key.NAME] rows (named keys) a topology may declare"
    range 1 64
    default 8 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 16
    help
      Size of the key table (types.rs, MAX_KEYS), CAPS.TOML format 8: each
      `[key.NAME]` row names a key by purpose and source. A row never holds
      key material.
      More rows are refused.

      Cost: 40 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_BOOT_STAGES
    int "[boot_chain.NAME] rows (boot stages) a topology may declare"
    range 1 16
    default 8
    help
      Size of the boot-chain table (types.rs, MAX_BOOT_STAGES), CAPS.TOML
      format 8: each `[boot_chain.NAME]` row is one stage of the boot chain and
      the digest it must measure to.
      More rows are refused.

      Cost: 56 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_ISLANDS
    int "[island.NAME] rows (low-power islands) a topology may declare"
    range 1 8
    default 1 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 2
    help
      Size of the island table (types.rs, MAX_ISLANDS), CAPS.TOML format 8:
      each `[island.NAME]` row is a co-processor that runs signed firmware over
      a mailbox queue.
      More rows are refused.

      Cost: 56 B of RAM per entry plus the wake list (TOPOLOGY_ISLAND_WAKE), either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_ISLAND_WAKE
    int "Wake events one [island.NAME] row may list"
    range 1 16
    default 4 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 8
    help
      Size of an island row's wake list (types.rs, ISLAND_WAKE), CAPS.TOML
      format 8: the events that wake the main kernel. More events are refused.
      More rows are refused.

      Cost: 16 B of RAM per event per island row, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_ACTIVITIES
    int "[activity.NAME] rows (deferred work) a topology may declare"
    range 1 64
    default 8 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 16
    help
      Size of the activity table (types.rs, MAX_ACTIVITIES), CAPS.TOML format
      8: each `[activity.NAME]` row names a task that runs when the machine
      can spare it.
      More rows are refused.

      Cost: 40 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_NUMA
    int "[numa.NAME] rows (memory nodes) a topology may declare"
    range 1 64
    default 2 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 8
    help
      Size of the NUMA table (types.rs, MAX_NUMA), CAPS.TOML format 8: each
      `[numa.NAME]` row is one memory node and the CPUs attached to it.
      More rows are refused.

      Cost: 32 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_ENVELOPES
    int "[envelope.NAME] rows (effect ceilings) a topology may declare"
    range 15 64
    default 16
    help
      Size of the effect-envelope table (types.rs, MAX_ENVELOPES), CAPS.TOML
      format 8: each `[envelope.NAME]` row bounds one kind of effect (config
      write, OTA apply, egress, delete, real-time reservation) for one class.
      More rows are refused. The built-in development topology states five
      `full` rows (delete, egress, config, ota, rt_time) for each of the three
      classes its tasks run in, 15 rows, so the table is not smaller than that
      on any profile.

      Cost: 40 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_NICS
    int "[nic.NAME] rows (network interfaces) a topology may declare"
    range 1 16
    default 2 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 4
    help
      Size of the NIC table (types.rs, MAX_NICS), CAPS.TOML format 8: each
      `[nic.NAME]` row configures one network device (MTU, address).
      More rows are refused.

      Cost: 48 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_MODELS
    int "[model.NAME] rows (model bundles) a topology may declare"
    range 1 32
    default 2 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 8
    help
      Size of the model table (types.rs, MAX_MODELS), CAPS.TOML format 8: each
      `[model.NAME]` row names a model bundle by hash and variant.
      More rows are refused.

      Cost: 64 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_USB_RULES
    int "[usb.NAME] rows (USB authorization rules) a topology may declare"
    range 1 64
    default 8 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 16
    help
      Size of the USB rule table (types.rs, MAX_USB_RULES), CAPS.TOML format
      8: each `[usb.NAME]` row allows or denies the USB devices it selects.
      Rules are tried in file order and the first match wins.
      More rows are refused.

      Cost: 32 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_GUEST_PASSTHROUGH
    int "Devices one [guest.NAME] row may pass through"
    range 1 16
    default 4 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 8
    help
      Size of a guest row's passthrough list (types.rs, GUEST_PASSTHROUGH),
      CAPS.TOML format 8: the physical devices handed to the guest itself.
      More devices are refused.
      More rows are refused.

      Cost: 16 B of RAM per device per guest row, either ISA.

config NAMES_LEND_TTL_MS
    int "Lifetime of a capability the name server lends (ms, 0 = the server's own)"
    range 0 86400000
    default 0 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 600000 if HWCLASS_SERVER
    default 3600000
    help
      The name server (NAMESD.ELF) stamps every endpoint it lends with
      `now + this`: the transfer descriptor's `expiry_ns`, which the kernel
      turns into the deadline of the borrower's copy in the warrant tree (the
      earlier of it and the server's own, so a lease never outlives its
      parent). When it passes, a call through the copy fails as stale and the
      borrower asks the name server again (azos_names::Renewing). 0 lends
      with no deadline of its own: the copy lives as long as the server's
      capability, which a static wiring (EMBEDDED) has no use to shorten.

      A shorter lease bounds how long a revoked or re-routed service stays
      reachable through an old copy; each renewal is one more name-server
      call per lease, so a very short one costs a lookup every few calls.
      Cost: no RAM; one clock read per lookup. The same on every ISA: it is a
      wall-clock lifetime, not a cycle count.

config TOPOLOGY_NAME_MAX
    int "Longest task, class or pipeline name in a topology (bytes)"
    range 8 255
    default 32
    help
      MAX_TASK_NAME_LEN: the longest section name ([task.NAME],
      [class.NAME], [pipeline.NAME]), class reference and enum word the
      parser accepts; longer is refused (NameTooLong). Names borrow from
      the file buffer, so this costs no RAM; it bounds the parser's scans.

config TOPOLOGY_TARGET_MAX
    int "Longest grant target in a topology (bytes)"
    range 16 255
    default 64
    help
      MAX_TARGET_LEN: the longest `target = "..."` of a grant (a channel
      path, an image name, "disk.part.1"); longer is refused. No RAM cost
      (targets borrow from the file buffer).

config TOPOLOGY_INLINE_FIELDS_MAX
    int "Fields in one grant's inline table"
    range 5 32
    default 8
    help
      MAX_INLINE_FIELDS: how many `key = value` pairs one `{ ... }` grant
      may hold (kind, target, perm, transfer, ops are the five in use). More
      are refused. Cost: 32 B of parser stack per field, either ISA.

choice
    prompt "CPU entropy instruction as a pool seed source"
    default ENTROPY_CPU_SOURCE_CREDIT
    help
      The CPU's own entropy instruction seeds the kernel entropy pool
      (kernel/src/boot/entropy.rs `install_entropy`, after virtio-rng and
      independent of it): riscv64 Zkr `seed` CSR (RV_ZKR), aarch64 FEAT_RNG
      RNDRRS (A64_RNG), x86_64 RDSEED, else RDRAND (X86_RDSEED,
      X86_RDRAND). Each instruction is used only where its own extension
      choice allows it and the boot probe finds it; without one the boot
      is exactly as before. The pool seeds the TCP ISN secret, the
      DHCP/DNS/NTP ids, the link keys, ring 3's `entropy` endpoint and
      the stack canary; without any credited source they stay on their
      runtime-counter paths.

      credit: mixed and credited, so the CPU alone seeds the pool on a
      board with no entropy device (Linux RANDOM_TRUST_CPU=y). mix-only:
      mixed, never credited; the pool still needs virtio-rng or the
      persisted seed (Linux RANDOM_TRUST_CPU=n). off: never read.

      Cost: one draw of 48 bytes at boot (RDSEED/RNDRRS: a few hundred
      cycles per 8 bytes; Zkr: 16 bits per CSR read); nothing after.

    config ENTROPY_CPU_SOURCE_CREDIT
        bool "credit: mix and credit (trust the CPU)"
    config ENTROPY_CPU_SOURCE_MIX
        bool "mix-only: mix, never credit"
    config ENTROPY_CPU_SOURCE_OFF
        bool "off: never read the CPU's entropy instruction"
endchoice

config ENTROPY_CPU_RETRIES
    int "CPU entropy instruction: retries per word"
    range 1 100000
    default 1000
    help
      How many times the boot retries one word of the CPU entropy
      instruction when it reports no data (x86 CF=0, aarch64 NZCV.Z=1,
      riscv64 BIST/WAIT) before it gives the source up for this boot, with
      the ISA's pause hint between tries. RDSEED and RNDRRS wait for a
      fresh conditioner output and can fail many times in a row under
      load; Intel's 10-retry guidance is for RDRAND only. Bounded either
      way: a dead source costs this many tries once, never a hang.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TPM 2.0 (crates/drivers/tpm, kernel/src/boot/tpm.rs)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# One native driver, three users: an entropy source for the pool, the boot
# measurements (PCR extends) and, when chosen below, the OTA anti-rollback
# floor. The TPM is found, never assumed: x86_64 reads the ACPI TPM2 table
# (TIS over MMIO), riscv64 and aarch64 read the device tree for a
# `tcg,tpm_tis-spi` node and talk to it through the `Spi` device interface.
# No TPM, or a TPM that does not answer, leaves the boot exactly as it was.

config TPM
    # cargo-feature: tpm
    bool "TPM 2.0 (entropy, measured boot, OTA rollback floor)"
    default y if !HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default n
    help
      Kernel feature `tpm`. At the entropy step of the boot the kernel
      looks for a TPM 2.0 (x86_64: the ACPI TPM2 table with the TIS/FIFO
      start method; riscv64, aarch64: a device-tree node compatible with
      `tcg,tpm_tis-spi`, reached through the `Spi` interface on bus
      TPM_SPI_BUS). It sends Startup (an already-started TPM is fine),
      draws random bytes into the entropy pool (ENTROPY_TPM_SOURCE), and
      extends each boot stage's digest into a PCR (TPM_PCR_BASE) while
      writing one decision record per extend. A CRB-only TPM (ACPI start
      methods 7 and 8) is reported and left alone. Polling driver: the
      boot waits on the TPM for at most the PTP timeouts (a few seconds)
      and only on a board that has one.

      Cost: no TPM present, a table lookup and a window probe at boot;
      with a TPM, a few commands at boot (milliseconds each). n: none of
      it is compiled.

choice
    prompt "TPM random bytes as a pool seed source"
    default ENTROPY_TPM_SOURCE_CREDIT
    depends on TPM
    help
      The TPM's GetRandom (48 bytes, two commands) is mixed into the
      kernel entropy pool (kernel/src/boot/entropy.rs `install_entropy`),
      after virtio-rng and the CPU instruction and independently of them.

      credit: mixed and credited, so a TPM alone seeds the pool on a board
      with no other source (Linux's hwrng gives the TPM a quality of
      1000). mix-only: mixed, never credited. off: never asked.

    config ENTROPY_TPM_SOURCE_CREDIT
        bool "credit: mix and credit (trust the TPM)"
    config ENTROPY_TPM_SOURCE_MIX
        bool "mix-only: mix, never credit"
    config ENTROPY_TPM_SOURCE_OFF
        bool "off: never ask the TPM for random bytes"
endchoice

config TPM_PCR_BASE
    int "First PCR of the boot measurements"
    depends on TPM
    range 0 21
    default 8
    help
      The boot stages are extended into three consecutive SHA-256 PCRs,
      in the order the boot learns them: TPM_PCR_BASE the kernel image
      (its build-id: SHA-256 of the linked text, LIVEPATCH on, else of the
      text as it stands once the TPM is found), +1 the signed
      configuration (CONFIG.INI as accepted), +2 the topology (CAPS.TOM as
      accepted, or the built-in topology's fixed name). PCRs 0-7 belong to
      firmware; 8 is where the OS-defined range starts, and a verifier
      that quotes PCRs 8-10 sees all three stages.

config TPM_SPI_BUS
    int "SPI bus the device-tree TPM sits on"
    depends on TPM && !ARCH_X86_64
    range 0 255
    default 0
    help
      The `Spi` interface names a device by bus and chip select. The tree
      gives the chip select (the node's `reg`) and the clock
      (`spi-max-frequency`) but not the driver's bus number, which is
      the SPI driver's own numbering; every board has one controller
      today, bus 0.

choice
    prompt "OTA anti-rollback floor (min_sec_epoch) backend"
    default OTA_FLOOR_BOOTMETA
    help
      Where the epoch floor of the whole-system update is held. A manifest
      whose sec_epoch is below the floor is refused whatever its
      signature, and the floor only ever goes up, when a boot is
      confirmed.

      BOOTMETA: the floor is a field of the BOOTMETA.A/.B records on the
      FAT volume (CRC and sequence number, no secret): whoever can write
      the volume (it is exported over USB mass storage) can write an older
      record back and lower the floor, and the signature check is then the
      only gate left. TPM NV: the floor is also a 4-byte NV index of the
      TPM, defined once and raised only upward (a read comes first and a
      value at or below the stored one is never written); the floor in
      force is the higher of the two, so replacing BOOTMETA no longer
      lowers it. The index is an ordinary one with a password the kernel
      derives (see kernel/src/boot/tpm.rs), so it is as safe as that
      password is secret; a policy bound to PCRs is the next step. With
      no TPM found, or one that fails, BOOTMETA's floor holds alone and
      the boot says so. (A replay-protected eMMC partition, RPMB, is the
      other hardware floor this choice has a place for; none is
      implemented.)

    config OTA_FLOOR_BOOTMETA
        bool "BOOTMETA: the FAT volume's boot record only"
    config OTA_FLOOR_TPM_NV
        bool "TPM NV: BOOTMETA and a TPM NV index, the higher wins"
        depends on TPM
endchoice

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ring-3 allocator (userspace/lib/ualloc)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The knobs of the hardened allocator native ring-3 programs link
# (`azos_ualloc`, Scudo's shape: size-class blocks, randomized chunk
# placement, checksummed chunk headers, a quarantine of freed chunks, guard
# pages). They are plain integers and bools read through `azos_limits`, not
# cargo features: the allocator is a userspace library, not a kernel feature.
# Defaults follow the SECURITY posture: every posture but `general` starts
# with the quarantine, the free-chunk poison and the randomized placement on.

config USER_ALLOC_QUARANTINE_KB
    int "Ring-3 allocator: quarantine of freed small chunks, KiB (0 = off)"
    range 0 4096
    default 64 if !SECURITY_GENERAL
    default 0
    help
      Total slot bytes the allocator holds back after a free before a chunk
      can be handed out again. A chunk in quarantine keeps its header in the
      "quarantined" state, so a second free of it is a detected double free,
      and (USER_ALLOC_POISON_FREED) a write through a stale pointer is
      detected when the chunk leaves quarantine. Chunks leave in random
      order when USER_ALLOC_RANDOMIZE is on, oldest first otherwise. 0
      turns the quarantine off: a freed chunk is reusable at once, and a
      double free is detected only until its chunk is allocated again.
      Cost: the bytes are resident while held (Scudo's default is 256 KiB
      per thread cache, this is per process). Large allocations are never
      quarantined: they are unmapped on free and their address range is
      never reused (the kernel's mmap only moves the break up and brk does
      not shrink it; a kernel that lowered the break would void this), so a
      stale pointer into one faults.

config USER_ALLOC_QUARANTINE_SLOTS
    int "Ring-3 allocator: quarantine entries"
    range 8 4096
    default 128
    help
      How many freed chunks the quarantine can hold (the table is static,
      8 bytes each). A free that finds the table full evicts one chunk
      first, whatever the byte budget says.

config USER_ALLOC_BLOCK_KB
    int "Ring-3 allocator: size-class block, KiB"
    range 8 1024
    default 16
    help
      How much anonymous memory a size class takes from the kernel each
      time it runs out of chunks. The kernel charges a mapping against the
      program's frame budget whole, when it is made, so a small block keeps
      a program with a tight `mem_pages` row inside it; a big one makes
      fewer mmap calls. Rounded up to the page size.

config USER_ALLOC_MAX_MAPS
    int "Ring-3 allocator: live mappings (blocks + large allocations)"
    range 16 4096
    default 256
    help
      Capacity of the table the allocator checks every freed pointer
      against (16 bytes each). A block or large allocation that does not
      fit fails the allocation (null), it is never untracked.

config USER_ALLOC_RANDOMIZE
    bool "Ring-3 allocator: randomize chunk placement"
    default y if !SECURITY_GENERAL
    default n
    help
      Which free chunk an allocation takes, the order a fresh block is
      handed out in, the position of a large allocation inside its pages
      and the quarantine eviction order come from the kernel entropy pool
      (SYS_ENTROPY_READ_TYPED, so the program's row needs the `entropy`
      capability). Without it the allocator is deterministic: LIFO reuse,
      fresh chunks in address order, large allocations ending exactly at
      their guard page (the tightest overflow detection). A program
      without the capability, or on a boot whose pool is unseeded, runs
      deterministic and prints one line saying so, unless
      USER_ALLOC_SEED_REQUIRED.

config USER_ALLOC_SEED_REQUIRED
    bool "Ring-3 allocator: fault when no kernel entropy is available"
    default y if SECURITY_SEPARATION_STATIC || SECURITY_CONFIDENTIAL
    default n
    help
      The header checksum is keyed and the placement randomized from one
      read of the entropy pool at the first allocation. With this on, a
      program that cannot get it (no `entropy` capability, unseeded pool)
      takes a recorded fault there instead of running with a key and a
      placement an attacker can predict.

config USER_ALLOC_POISON_FREED
    bool "Ring-3 allocator: poison freed chunks and check the poison"
    default y if !SECURITY_GENERAL
    default n
    help
      A freed chunk's first 512 bytes are filled with a fixed byte and
      checked when it leaves quarantine (or, without quarantine, when it is
      handed out again): a write through a stale pointer is a recorded
      fault, not silent corruption. Cost: a fill and a compare of up to 512
      bytes per free.

config USER_ALLOC_GUARD_PAGES
    bool "Ring-3 allocator: guard pages around blocks and large allocations"
    default y
    help
      Every block and every large allocation is mapped between two
      PROT_NONE reservations (mmap with prot 0: the range is reserved, no
      page is mapped, a touch kills the task). Costs address space and two
      extra mmap calls per mapping, no frames. Off: blocks and large
      allocations are mapped back to back.

# ── Native SDK runtime (sdk/, crates/core/stdrt) ─────────────────────────────
# A plain integer read by `make sdk` (tools/kconfig_to_cargo.py --sdk-env), like
# the allocator knobs above, whose engine `std::alloc::System` shares.

config SDK_THREAD_STACK_KB
    int "Native SDK: default stack of a std thread, KiB"
    range 16 4096
    default 128
    help
      The stack `std::thread::spawn` gives a thread when the caller names no
      size. The kernel charges a mapping against the program's frame budget
      whole, when it is made, so a program with a tight `mem_pages` row wants
      this small; a deep recursion wants it large. Rounded up to the page
      size, with a guard page below and above.

config CAP_WEAK
    bool "Weak (transitively read-only) capabilities for shadow rows"
    default y
    help
      A topology task row with `shadow = true` (CAPS.TOML format 7) is
      admitted in observe-only mode: its capability table is marked weak
      (crates/core/ipc/src/cap_store.rs, mark_weak), so every capability it
      holds, however it came by it, resolves with its rights masked to READ,
      and everything derived from one stays weak (Warrant::WEAK, which no
      derive can clear). Each use the mask refuses is recorded as a
      shadow-refusal decision (DECISION_RECORDS_DEV), the evidence for
      granting the authority by editing the signed row. The mask itself is
      one flag test in the slot word every resolve already loads.

      n: no weak warrant is ever published and a shadow row is seeded with
      no capabilities at all (it cannot run strong).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signed kernel livepatch (Level 2 live update; kernel/src/boot/livepatch.rs)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config LIVEPATCH
    bool "Signed kernel livepatch of non-sleeping functions"
    depends on RCU_QSBR && LIVEPATCH_POOL_KIB != 0 && LIVEPATCH_ENTRY_NOPS != 0
    default n
    help
      Admits a signed patch blob (a replacement body per function) named by
      a `[livepatch.NAME]` row of the signed topology, and redirects the
      entry of each function it replaces: one `B` on aarch64, one `JAL` on
      riscv64 (within 1 MiB of the pool), the `int3` sequence on x86_64.
      The bodies go into `.text.patch_pool` through the one text writer
      (crates/core/mm/src/text_poke.rs), so the kernel text stays RX and
      `verify_no_exec_outside_image` is unchanged. A blob is applied only
      when: its RSIG signature (the update key) verifies, a row names its
      SHA-256, it is built for this ISA and this kernel's build-id
      (`[PATCH] build=` on the boot line, tools/check_livepatch_layout.py),
      its `sec_epoch` is not below the OTA floor, and every function is in
      the image's allowlist (non-sleeping, `#[inline(never)]` entry points
      with an entry slot in `.azos_patch`). One bounded QSBR grace follows
      the redirect (LIVEPATCH_GRACE_MS); no CPU is ever stopped. Nothing
      persists across a reboot: the signed rows are re-applied at boot.

      n (default, and what a BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE build gets unless it
      asks otherwise): the code is not compiled in, the build-id is not
      computed, and a topology that declares `livepatch` rows has each one
      refused at boot with a decision record. y needs the pool
      (LIVEPATCH_POOL_KIB) and the entry slots (LIVEPATCH_ENTRY_NOPS: 1
      on aarch64, 3 on riscv64 where functions are 2-aligned, and on x86_64
      one 5-byte nop) in config/Kconfig.development.

      Cost with y: one SHA-256 pass over the whole kernel text (about 2 MiB)
      at boot to get the build-id, its time to be measured in the testing
      week; and the patch table (LIVEPATCH_MAX_PATCHES), 8 B plus
      8 + 8 * LIVEPATCH_MAX_FUNCS B per entry. Without it: 8 B.

config LIVEPATCH_GRACE_MS
    int "Livepatch: the bounded grace period after a redirect (ms)"
    range 1 10000
    default 200
    help
      How long an apply waits for every other CPU to pass a
      quiescent state (qsbr::synchronize_rcu_for) before it records the old
      body as possibly still running. The redirect is already in force and
      a miss does not undo it. Set it above the longest time a CPU runs kernel
      code without a context switch, an interrupt return or idle (an RT CPU
      in user mode is quiescent at once). Unused without LIVEPATCH.

config LIVEPATCH_MAX_PATCHES
    int "Livepatch: patches applied at once"
    range 1 32
    default 4
    help
      Size of the patch table, which holds the entries each patch has
      claimed (before its first write, and while it is in force). A patch
      that does not fit is refused. Never useful above the topology's
      `[livepatch.NAME]` rows (TOPOLOGY_MAX_LIVEPATCHES).

      Cost: 8 + 8 * LIVEPATCH_MAX_FUNCS B of RAM per entry (72 B with the
      default of 8 functions), either ISA; none without LIVEPATCH.

config LIVEPATCH_MAX_FUNCS
    int "Livepatch: functions one patch may replace"
    range 1 64
    default 8
    help
      Bound on the function table of one blob; a blob that names more is
      refused as malformed. Unused without LIVEPATCH.

config LIVEPATCH_MAX_BLOB_KIB
    int "Livepatch: largest patch blob (KiB)"
    range 1 1024
    default 64
    help
      The boot re-apply reads a blob from `/fat/PATCH/` into a buffer of
      this size, taken from the kernel heap for the read and given back
      after it; a larger file is refused. The sum of the bodies of the
      applied patches is bounded by LIVEPATCH_POOL_KIB instead. Unused
      without LIVEPATCH.

config DOMAIN_ENTRY_COUNTERS
    bool "Count kernel entries per Domain by foundational case"
    default y
    help
      The one prologue of the trap layer (kernel/src/trap/syscall.rs) counts
      every syscall in its Domain, in the counter of the call's foundational
      case (1 authority, 2 block or wake; azos_abi::syscall_nr::syscall_case;
      a device or cross-space op of invoke counts as authority), and in a
      third counter the calls of a Linux task, which have no case. The
      kernel-fault report prints them: what the task did with the kernel
      before it died. Off: no counter is kept and the report prints zeros.

      Cost: one relaxed atomic add on a counter shared by the threads of the
      Domain, on every syscall.

# MITIGATION_SPECTRE_V1_INDEX, INFER_MODEL_SIG_REQUIRED and CONSOLE_LOCKDOWN moved
# to the Mitigations menu (config/Kconfig.mitigations), wave 11.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hardware control-flow integrity (plan item I4)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# One model on every ISA: forward edges (an indirect call or jump may only
# land on a landing pad) and backward edges (a return address is signed or
# kept on a shadow stack). The per-ISA never/probe/require choices already
# exist next to the other ISA extensions (config/Kconfig.arch):
#
#   aarch64  A64_BTI (landing pads)        A64_PAUTH (signed returns)
#   x86_64   X86_CET_IBT (ENDBR64)         X86_CET_SHSTK (shadow stack)
#   riscv64  none yet (Zicfilp, below)
#
# Any policy but `n` makes the kernel AND the user images compile with the
# pads (tools/kconfig_to_cargo.py, cfi_rustflags: the pads are architectural
# no-ops on a CPU without the extension); the boot then uses the extension
# when the CPU has it (probe) or refuses the CPU (require). Kconfig is the
# only source: there is no second switch. The aarch64 signed returns are a
# ring-3/kernel boundary only with A64_PAUTH_PROC_KEYS (per-process keys, the
# kernel's key restored on every entry from EL0; A64_PAUTH help).
#
# Not done, and why (the reasons are what a later change starts from):
#   * x86_64 kernel shadow stack (X86_CET_SHSTK): the policy and the CPUID
#     row exist, the kernel does not turn it on. It needs a shadow-stack page
#     set per kernel task and per IST stack, IA32_PL0_SSP switched in
#     `context_switch`, and the retpoline thunk (a `ret` that does not match
#     its `call`) off, i.e. X86_MIT_SPECTRE_V2 not `probe` on a CPU that
#     lacks eIBRS/AutoIBRS. Ring 3 shadow stacks come with the per-process
#     CET state (XSAVES supervisor component).
#   * aarch64 GCS (FEAT_GCS, Armv9.4): reserved. The return-address stack is
#     the aarch64 twin of the shadow stack; GCSCR_EL1/GCSPR_EL1 would be
#     programmed in crates/core/arch-aarch64/src/cpu/cfi.rs. No compiler mode
#     is needed beyond `-Z branch-protection=gcs`, which the pinned
#     compiler does not take.
#   * riscv64 Zicfilp (`lpad` at every function entry): no symbol until the
#     boot can use it. The compiler half exists (`-Z cf-protection=branch`
#     plus the LLVM feature `experimental-zicfilp`), but the pinned compiler
#     (rustc 1.95 nightly of 2026-02-21, LLVM 22.1) lists the feature as
#     unknown and unstable and warns once per crate, which tools/ci_check.sh
#     fails on; and nothing in the tree could enforce or refuse it: a hart
#     declares `zicfilp` in the device tree, but the S-mode enable belongs to
#     M-mode (menvcfg.LPE; SBI FWFT feature LANDING_PAD), so it needs the
#     device-tree probe, an arch-api policy constant, a riscv64 `[ISA]` row,
#     the FWFT call, `lpad` in the riscv64 entry assembly and a host row. A
#     choice whose `require` could not refuse would only mislead. Zicfiss
#     (shadow stack) needs a shadow-stack CSR switched in `context_switch`
#     and `sspush`/`sspopchk`, illegal on a hart without Zimop.
#   * kCFI (`-Z sanitizer=kcfi`, the software forward-edge check for CPUs
#     without BTI/IBT/Zicfilp): the pinned compiler takes it for aarch64
#     and x86_64 and refuses riscv64 ("kcfi sanitizer is not supported for
#     this target"). Not wired: every indirect call into hand-written asm
#     would need the type hash the compiler puts before a function, and the
#     trap needs its own decoder. Without a user it stays a note.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Allocator hardening (audit findings D and E, allocator side)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config SLAB_HARDENED
    bool "Harden the slab and buddy free lists (obfuscated links, safe unlink)"
    default y
    help
      Slab: the link a freed object keeps in its first word is stored as
      next ^ secret ^ byteswap(address of the word) (Linux SLAB_FREELIST_
      HARDENED), with one secret per slab page. Every pop checks that the
      decoded link is null or the start of an object inside the same slab;
      every free checks that the pointer is an object of the slab and is not
      the one freed last. The list of slabs with room is unlinked only if
      prev->next == self and next->prev == self. Buddy: a block leaves its
      free list only if both neighbours link back to it and its descriptor
      still names the order of the list. Each failure panics with the
      addresses involved (the panic record is the crash-log entry).
      Release builds included: nothing here is a debug_assert.

      On in every build, certifiable ones too: the checks and the secret
      never change which object or block an allocation returns, so they
      cost no determinism. Cost: one xor per link, one range test and one
      remainder per slab pop and free, two loads per buddy unlink.
      Secret source: the entropy pool, drawn with interrupts on when a slab
      page is created and the pool is seeded; before that, or when the page
      is created with interrupts masked, the timer tick mixed with the
      page address. A weak secret still defeats a blind overwrite of a link
      but not an attacker who knows the boot timing; the pool-seeded ones
      are not guessable.

config ALLOC_RANDOM
    bool "Randomize allocation order (slab carve order, buddy list order)"
    depends on KHEAP_SLAB
    default n if BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE || SECURITY_SEPARATION_STATIC
    default y
    help
      Slab (Linux SLAB_FREELIST_RANDOM): the objects of a new slab page are
      first handed out in a random order (a random start and a random step
      coprime with the object count, drawn per page) instead of ascending
      addresses, so the neighbour of an object cannot be predicted from the
      allocation sequence. Buddy: once the entropy pool has been drawn
      (the slab layer feeds the page allocator a word with every slab page
      it creates), each free list is rotated by a random number of blocks
      once, and every block pushed after that goes to the head or the tail
      of its list on a random bit. Blocks pushed before the first draw
      (early boot) keep the plain order.

      Default n when BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE or SECURITY_SEPARATION_STATIC
      (the industrial and automotive presets): there the same inputs must
      give the same addresses and the same latency on every boot, and
      randomized placement is the one thing here that breaks it. y
      everywhere else. Depends on KHEAP_SLAB because the seed for the page
      allocator rides on slab page creation.
      Cost: a few instructions per slab page created and per buddy push;
      the order of free lists after reclaim differs from boot to boot.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Kernel address-space layout randomization (virtual KASLR)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where the slide exists: every ISA, except an image that has no MMU to map it
# at another address (riscv64 on a small board, HWCLASS_EMBEDDED, runs in the
# firmware's identity map) and the x86_64 Limine image, which the loader
# places itself (X86_BOOT_LIMINE; its ELF is not position-independent and the
# loader's own KASLR is off for it). A choice hidden by `depends on` reads as
# `probe`, so the Rust side asks this symbol first (azos_arch_api::kaslr).
config KASLR_AVAILABLE
    def_bool ARCH_AARCH64 || (ARCH_RISCV64 && !HWCLASS_EMBEDDED) || (ARCH_X86_64 && !X86_BOOT_LIMINE)

choice
    prompt "Kernel address-space layout randomization (KASLR)"
    depends on KASLR_AVAILABLE
    default KASLR_REQUIRE if BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE || SECURITY_HARDENED
    default KASLR_PROBE
    help
      Where the kernel image sits in virtual memory. Any policy but n
      links the kernel position-independent (a `-pie` link, kernel/build.rs,
      with the code PC-relative, tools/kconfig_to_cargo.py kaslr_rustflags;
      a boot that finds no relocation table refuses to slide): the boot
      picks a random base for the image inside the ISA's image window
      (crates/core/arch-*/src/mmu/kaslr.rs), maps the image there and applies
      the image's relative relocations (`R_*_RELATIVE`) with the distance from
      the link address, before any Rust that needs a pointer runs. A leaked
      kernel pointer then no longer says where the rest of the image is, and a
      fixed-address gadget or a fixed-address data target is gone.

      What moves and what does not: only the image. The map of RAM keeps its
      address (`phys_to_virt` stays one add or one OR: aarch64's linear map,
      x86_64's direct map, riscv64's identity) and the image window does not
      overlap it, so a slid image is a second mapping of its own frames; the
      RAM map's alias of the image is made read-only and not executable at boot
      (kernel/src/boot/early.rs). The direct map's base is not randomized: it
      would turn every `phys_to_virt` into a load of a runtime base.

      | ISA     | window                                   | step   | bits (30 MiB image) | seed                       |
      | aarch64 | the upper half of the TTBR1 range        | 2 MiB* | ~16                 | RNDRRS, device tree        |
      | riscv64 | the Sv39 upper half, 256 GiB             | 2 MiB  | ~16                 | Zkr `seed` CSR, device tree|
      | x86_64  | the first GiB of the top 2 GiB           | 2 MiB  | ~9 (8 whole)        | RDSEED, RDRAND             |

      (*) 2 MiB at a 4 KiB granule; 16 KiB pages slide in 32 MiB steps, 64 KiB
      pages in 512 MiB steps (tests/host/kaslr-tests assert the counts).
      x86_64 is held to the top 2 GiB by `-mcmodel=kernel`, and its AP start
      tables map one GiB of image; Linux has the same 9 bits there. The
      physical place of the image does not move (PVH loads it at 1 MiB).

      The seed is the CPU's source (RNDRRS with A64_RNG, the `seed` CSR with
      RV_ZKR, RDSEED then RDRAND with X86_RDSEED / X86_RDRAND, each unless its
      own choice is n), mixed with the device tree's `/chosen/kaslr-seed` and
      `/chosen/rng-seed` when the loader supplies them (x86_64's PVH start-info
      carries no tree). The boot erases those properties after reading them, as
      Linux does. The seed is what `probe` looks for and `require` insists on.

      n: the link address is the run address, the image is not
      position-independent and the boot code is not built. probe: slide when
      a seed exists, else run at the link address (the boot says so on the
      console). require: refuse to boot without a seed or a slot, before the
      console exists (the PL011 on aarch64, SBI's console on riscv64, COM1 on
      x86_64).

      Cost: the relocation table is part of the image (24 bytes per
      pointer-bearing word: tens of KiB), the boot applies it once (a few
      hundred thousand cycles), and position-independent code loads statics
      through PC-relative addressing as before while function and vtable
      tables become relocated data. Not a cost at run time otherwise, except
      that `virt_to_phys` of an address in the image window takes the image's
      recorded base instead of the one constant (an add and a compare more).

      The default is require for BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE and for
      SECURITY_HARDENED (an image that runs third-party code, or is certified,
      must not run at a fixed layout by accident), probe everywhere else,
      BUILD_TYPE_DEV included: a development image slides like a release one,
      so a layout bug shows up where it is written, not on the first release
      build. A debugger or a trace that needs fixed addresses sets n by hand
      (the gate's `plain` KASLR rows do, tools/kaslr_image.sh). The slide
      itself is printed only on a BUILD_TYPE_DEV image; other builds print that
      the image is slid and by how many bits.

      Not available (the choice is hidden, KASLR_AVAILABLE): riscv64 on a
      small board (HWCLASS_EMBEDDED), whose image runs in the firmware's
      identity map without an MMU of its own, and the x86_64 Limine image,
      which the loader places.

    config KASLR_NEVER
        bool "n: link address = run address"
    config KASLR_PROBE
        bool "probe: slide when the boot finds a seed, else run at the link address"
    config KASLR_REQUIRE
        bool "require: refuse to boot without a seed"
endchoice

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Keystore (azos_crypto::keystore, the kernel-hosted `keys` interface)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Keys held for their users: a task holds a handle and asks the kernel to
# sign, seal or derive with it (`azos_devif::keys`, `azos_syscall::keys`). The
# table is a fixed array in .bss; the roots persistent keys derive from are
# the board seed (BOOTMETA's reserved sector, always), a secret in a TPM NV
# index, and a secret in a file on a sealed volume.

config KEYSTORE_KEYS
    int "Keystore: key handles the kernel holds"
    range 4 255
    default 16 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 64 if HWCLASS_SERVER
    default 32
    help
      Live handles in all owners' tables together (a handle names its slot
      in 8 bits, so at most 255). A slot is about 130 bytes of .bss. A
      handle that expired or was destroyed gives its slot back; a task that
      exits does not destroy the keys of its owner, so a service that opens
      the same named key again gets the same handle and a service that
      makes volatile keys destroys them or gives them a time to live.

config KEYSTORE_KEYS_PER_OWNER
    int "Keystore: handles one owner may hold"
    range 1 255
    default 4 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 8
    help
      One owner (a user, `owner_root`; 0 is the system) cannot fill the
      table: past this share a new key is refused with BUSY. At most
      KEYSTORE_KEYS.

config KEYSTORE_TPM_NV
    bool "Keystore: a secret in a TPM NV index as a root (backend tpm_nv)"
    depends on TPM
    default y
    help
      The boot reads a 32-byte secret from an NV index of the TPM (defined
      and filled from the entropy pool the first time), and keys opened
      with the tpm_nv backend are derived from it: they exist only on a
      board with this TPM, and the volume holds nothing to copy. The index
      has the OTA floor's shape and the same password rule (a password the
      kernel derives, so it is as safe as that password is secret; a policy
      bound to PCRs is the next step). With no TPM, or one that fails, the
      backend answers NOT_READY and the boot says so.

config KEYSTORE_VOLUME_ROOT_PATH
    string "Keystore: file holding the secret of the volume backend"
    default ""
    help
      The path of a 32-byte file, on a volume the kernel mounts, that keys
      opened with the volume backend derive from; empty: no volume backend
      (it answers NOT_READY). The file is created from the entropy pool at
      the first boot that can write it, after the mounts. Put it under the
      mount point of an `aead = true` [volume.NAME] row (azvol, sealed under
      the board's seed), so that the secret is ciphertext on the medium and
      a volume cloned to another board does not open; on a plain FAT32
      volume it is readable by whoever can read the medium.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Console login (the `login` op of the `keys` interface; userspace/services/logind)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# CONSOLE_LOGIN itself is in Kconfig.limits (it decides whether the native shell
# starts at boot). These bound the kernel's side of the credential check.

config LOGIN_MAX_FAILURES
    int "Console login: wrong credentials in a row before the account is locked"
    range 1 32
    default 5
    help
      The `login` op of `keys` counts the consecutive refusals of each
      `[login.NAME]` row (and, in one more counter, of every name the topology
      does not list). At this many the account is locked for LOGIN_LOCKOUT_S
      seconds: the op answers DENIED at once, without looking at the
      credential, and writes a `login` decision record (reason 2). A good
      credential before the limit clears the count. The counters are kept in
      RAM, so a reboot clears them.

config LOGIN_LOCKOUT_S
    int "Console login: seconds an account stays locked"
    range 1 3600
    default 30
    help
      How long a locked account refuses every credential. The refusal at the
      lock is the last one counted; the next attempt after the lock lapses
      starts the count again.

config LOGIN_GRANT_TTL_S
    int "Console login: seconds a verified credential may start its session"
    range 1 600
    default 20
    help
      A credential that verifies arms a one-shot grant on the caller's Domain
      (the login service); its next SPAWN_F_USER spawn starts the session and
      spends it. A grant not spent in this many seconds lapses, so a login
      service that stalls cannot sit on an admitted credential.

endmenu # Security
